  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: explain how relational database executes SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\n\nNeed produce final answer. Must be thorough. Need likely structure: overview pipeline: tokenizer/parser, binder/resolver, optimizer (logical/physical), executor, storage engine, concurrency control, network. Include examples SQL: orders, customers, products. Discuss parse, syntax tree, name resolution, permissions. Planner: logical plan, statistics, cardinality estimates, cost model, physical operators, join orders, index selection, access paths. Indices: B-tree, hash, covering, composite, selectivity, SARGable, covering indexes, index-only scans, index nested loops, merge join, hash join. Tradeoffs: write overhead, index bloat, stale stats, wrong plans. Transactions: MVCC, ACID, isolation levels, locking, dirty reads, nonrepeatable, phantoms, snapshot isolation, read committed, serializable, locking isolation, deadlocks, lock escalation, write skew, long transactions. Returning rows: fetch protocol, row formatting, cursor/paging, result caching maybe not. Performance pitfalls: N+1, full scans, implicit casts, leading wildcard LIKE %, functions in predicate, OR, UNION misuse, ORDER BY/GROUP BY sort, LIMIT with offset, high cardinality estimates, small tables, missing indexes, over-indexing, bad data types, temp tables, spooling, parallelism. Need include concrete examples and tradeoffs.\n\nNeed avoid repeating. Can use headings. Need complete sentences. Maybe include a running example:\n\nCREATE TABLE customers (customer_id int PK, region text, name text);\nCREATE TABLE orders (order_id int PK, customer_id int FK, status text, order_date date, total numeric);\nCREATE TABLE order_items ...? Maybe not.\n\nQuery:\nSELECT c.name, o.order_id, o.total\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'shipped'\n  AND o.order_date >= date '2025-01-01'\nORDER BY o.total DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n\nExplain parse: tokenization etc. Bound query.\n\nPlanner: stats, cost. Access path choices: nested loop with index on orders(order_date, status?) or customers PK. If",
